{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'untrusted|forg|capability|literal|:vm/resume|continuation' src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md docs/design/yin.vm* | head -95\"","aggregated_output":"docs/design/yin.vm-in-dao.space.md:14:Memory allocation is no longer requesting bytes from an OS heap; it is appending a generative tuple to a local `dao.stream` and instantly updating the local B-Tree index. The CESK machine's \"memory\" is literally a database index running in the same process as the execution loop.\ndocs/design/yin.vm-in-dao.space.md:38:Because the local, in-memory `dao.space.index` shares the exact structural semantics as the persistent segments in `dao.jing`, migrating or parking a continuation becomes trivial. There is no complex serialization step. You simply flush the local B-Tree segments to `dao.jing` as content-addressed blobs. The parked continuation is just a root hash pointing to that index.\ndocs/design/yin.vm-in-dao.space.md:44:Because the parked continuation (`K`) and its control pointer (`C`) are queryable datoms, \"waking up\" an agent requires no central scheduler. A worker node runs a continuous Datalog query against the tuple space: *\"Find me any Continuation that is ready to execute.\"*\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:20:3. Executing the continuation CESK loop.\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:70:| integer     | entity ids, `t`, and literals share this type             |\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:79:Closures and continuations are **not** host values. A closure is an entity id;\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:80:a continuation is an entity id. They travel in ref attributes (`:cell/ref`,\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:117:    :cfg/kont      ref to a continuation chain (:k/tag, :k/next, ...)\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:122:    :literal :variable :lambda :application :if :let :letrec :out :rd\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:152:Parking and resuming continuations is not on this list: blocking is part of\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:185:the integer type with scalar literals, so the differ must know the closed set\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:188:only those values; a blind integer shift cannot tell a ref from a literal.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:18:In datom.world, code and continuations flow through streams: one universal\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:315:   parks its continuation and emits a request carrying the identity (H or R).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:317:   `fetch`. All request tokens, continuations, and response outcomes are pure\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:398:- Provide cross-model continuation transport.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:11:[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:131:matters more than any item:** `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` is\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:230:  `:current-continuation → :current-cont` alias is missing from §2.4's\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:365:row whose `data` slot carries a metadata-bearing literal materializes,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:562:the hand fixture; a two-tree relation with the same literal path in both\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:564:`:if`, `:vm/resume`, and the `:stream/*` tags. Size: two to three days.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:602:corpus tree and vector on all three hosts; a metadata-bearing literal\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:757:literal's metadata.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:11:| `:vm/current-continuation` | `ast_walker.cljc:396-400` | Captures the current continuation as `{:type :reified-continuation, :k k, :env env}` |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:12:| `:vm/park` | `ast_walker.cljc:402-413` | Suspends the current computation, stores continuation in `:parked` map, halts. Returns the parked continuation descriptor as the VM's value. |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:13:| `:vm/resume` | `ast_walker.cljc:415-428` | Restores a parked continuation with a given value. |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:14:| `stream/next` (parking) | `ast_walker.cljc:143-164` | Parks the continuation in the wait-set when a stream is empty. |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:15:| `stream/put` (parking) | `ast_walker.cljc:104-126` | Parks the continuation in the wait-set when a stream is at capacity. |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:66:`:vm/park` suspends the current computation and returns the parked continuation descriptor as the VM's final value. The host (or a higher-level scheduler) inspects this value, extracts the park-id, and constructs a `:vm/resume` AST node for the next cycle. VM state carries across `load-program`/`run` cycles.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:73:         -> VM halts, value = {:type :parked-continuation, :id :parked-0, ...}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:75:Cycle 2: load {:type :vm/resume, :parked-id :parked-0, :val :go}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:77:         -> VM halts, value = {:type :parked-continuation, :id :parked-1, ...}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:81:         -> VM halts, value = {:type :parked-continuation, :id :parked-2, ...}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:83:Cycle 4: load {:type :vm/resume, :parked-id :parked-1, :val result-from-B}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:87:The host decides which parked continuation to resume and what value to pass. This is the most explicit model: the scheduling policy lives outside the VM.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:92:- Parked continuations are values in the VM store, inspectable and serializable\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:95:**Trade-off:** `:vm/resume` currently takes static `:parked-id` and `:val` fields baked into the AST node. The host constructs these after seeing the park result, so this works. For in-VM resume with runtime-computed park-ids, `:vm/resume` would need to evaluate its arguments as sub-expressions (same pattern as `:stream/put` evaluating `:target` and `:val`). This is a behavioral change to an existing node type, not a new node type.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:97:### 3. call/cc via current-continuation + park/resume (works today)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:99:`:vm/current-continuation` captures the current continuation as a reified value. Combined with park and resume, this gives call/cc semantics without a dedicated call/cc node type.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:104:;; Capture continuation, store it, park\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:105:(let [k (current-continuation)]    ;; k = {:type :reified-continuation, ...}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:110:(let [k (store-get :saved-k)]      ;; retrieve saved continuation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:114:The continuation is a first-class value. It can be stored, passed through streams, or sent to another node (via datom migration). The combination of capture + store + park + resume gives the same power as Scheme's `call/cc`.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:116:**Invoking reified continuations as functions:** The application dispatch of every VM has a `:reified-continuation` clause, so captured continuations are callable as functions (abortive, multi-shot, store not rolled back, exactly one argument; see `ast.md` Part 7), enabling the classic pattern:\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:127:`:vm/current-continuation`, e.g. `((fn [k] ... (k 42)) (current-continuation))`,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:131:- Maximum flexibility (full continuation control)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:152:Park/resume adds explicit process suspension, analogous to the pi-calculus restriction operator (creating a private channel/continuation).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:154:call/cc adds first-class continuations as mobile values, enabling dynamic process topology changes (new communication patterns created at runtime).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:158:All three models work today with the AST Walker VM. The other VMs (Stack, Register, Semantic) need the primitives added first (see `docs/continuation-stream-feature-parity-across-vms.md`).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:161:- Make `:vm/resume` evaluate its arguments as sub-expressions (enables in-VM resume with runtime-computed park-ids)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:22:[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:124:  closure, or a continuation.** The datom projection's `e` values are\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:152:        :operands [{:type :variable, :name x} {:type :literal, :value 1}],\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:172:[E :literal 1]          ; A..E are :segment/sha256-… row ids\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:213:position; it is never inferred from the value. A literal whose value happens\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:269:| :literal                | 2          | [:literal value]                     | value                    | data             | ast_walker.cljc:360| v2.cljc:414-415 |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:288:| :vm/current-continuation| 1          | [:vm/current-continuation]           | —                        | —                | :422-427           | :474-475        |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:292:| :vm/resume              | 3          | [:vm/resume parked-id val]           | parked-id val            | kw, node         | :431-438           | :470-473        |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:314:codec emits it as `:yin/value` (`v2.cljc:449`). The `:vm/resume` value slot\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:321:§2.4). The yang frontends mark `:tail? tr